{"kind":"request","text":"Planner, for hugh: decide where an application installs Artroom's packages from, and commission making them installable. This owes a decision only: no Git artifact, no landing.\n\nWhy now. My readiness judgment 77a2aada names two things the first jam task lacks. One is the code on main and deployed. The other is this: the jam may depend on Artroom only through installable packages and a deployed room, and no package can be installed from outside the repository.\n\nWhat I found, by experiment at a10c9bec (notes/2026-10-04-package-availability.md at 4cb7688e on request/test-overhead; nothing was published and nothing in the repository changed). Nothing is published, and the scope @generalbusiness does not exist on the public registry. Packed as they are, only the artroom command works from outside, because it is one bundled file. Every library fails from plain Node, because each package's exports point at TypeScript source and Node does not strip types under node_modules; a TypeScript consumer with ordinary settings gets 43 errors from Artroom's own source. A build that emits JavaScript and declarations, tried on scratch copies of contract, policy, client and mcp with no source change, fixes both.\n\nThe decision that is not mine. Publishing is outward-facing and needs the owner's registry account. The choices: (a) publish to the public registry under a scope the owner creates, with real versions; (b) give the jam packed tarballs attached to a release or a commit of this repository, and no registry yet; (c) another registry. For the first jam task (b) is enough and reversible; (a) is what request f3299ab4 names as its dependency.\n\nWhat I propose to build once that is decided, as its own request with its own review: built output and an exports map for contract, policy, client, mcp and log; matching versions between the packages; the command's bundled dependencies moved out of its install; a files list, a licence file, engines and public access for each; and one test that packs the packages, installs them in a fresh project and imports each from plain Node, so they stay installable. The starter and its conformance route stay with f3299ab4.","body":{"conditions":"(1) The owner's decision on where packages are installed from for the first jam task, and whether and when the public registry is used. (2) The packaging work is commissioned as a request with conditions, or folded into a named existing request. (3) If neither can be decided now, what the first jam task may use meanwhile.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}